  (server/sources/** -> sources/, shared/sources.json -> registry.json)

What the port changed, and why:

- The Nuxt build injected defineSource, myFetch, $fetch, rss2json, parseRelativeDate,
  tranformToUTC, md5, myCrypto and encodeBase64 into every adapter file. They live in runtime.ts
  now, and each adapter imports the ones it uses. Other changes are listed below.
- Requests use the existing guarded HTTP boundary (../fetch.ts): DNS and redirects are checked,
  response size and total time are bounded, and credentials do not cross origins.
- The dates are read by the engine's own readers (@aihot/backend/sources/dates) instead of dayjs:
  a relative date ("3 minutes ago", "yesterday 15:46") and a calendar date in the source's own
  offset are read the same way on every host. The three adapters that used dayjs directly
  (_36kr.ts, qqvideo.ts, producthunt.ts) use the engine's Beijing date and the zone helper in
  ../../dates.ts instead.
- zaobao.ts decoded its GB2312 page with iconv-lite; Node's own TextDecoder does the same.
- weibo.ts carried another person's session cookie (a hardcoded SUB= value). The hot list is
  published as JSON for every reader (weibo.com/ajax/side/hotSearch), so the adapter reads that; the
  words are the same the page prints. No credential of any person is in this folder.
- aihot.ts read the public instance's feed with a reader id in the query; the id is gone.
- A body that is JSON but is sent as plain text is read as JSON: some sites (Bilibili's hot words)
  answer that way, and the adapters expect the object.
- Steam's player ranking no longer labels the read time as publication time. V2EX and RSS use the
  original publication time rather than the modification time; an unknown time stays unknown.
- kuaishou.ts requests the HTML with a browser-compatible user agent; its endpoint otherwise
  returns a JSON response instead of the page the upstream parser expects.
- linuxdo.ts can read its public lists through this site's restricted BrowserMCP bridge when
  configured; otherwise it keeps the upstream JSON request. No browser cookies leave the host.
- Upstream's defineRSSHubSource (server/utils/source.ts) is not here: no adapter uses it, and it
  would add a dependency on a third party's public RSSHub instance.

Six adapters are not in upstream's registry (ghxi, smzdm, linuxdo, linuxdo-latest, linuxdo-hot,
pcbeta-windows): their names and addresses are in ../registry.ts, so they can be read and shown like
the others. Direct Linux DO requests can return 403; the optional browser bridge reads the public
lists instead. The Windows board can return a challenge page; failures remain visible to the reader.
